markdown <<MARKDOWN
# The Project Pipeline

One example runs through Lesson 13's own teaching materials, used to demonstrate each step of the pipeline: a conversational AI that recommends a class to take, based on a student's answers. Your students will each choose their own topic, this is just the example the lesson itself uses to walk through how the pipeline works.


<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128214; Conversational AI:</strong> a technology that lets users communicate with software through natural language.
  </div>
</div>

## 1. Choose a Topic

Students brainstorm what their conversational AI could help someone decide, then narrow to specific decisions it could suggest. The running example: "Do you like sea animals?" leads to Marine Biology, "Do you like music more than acting?" branches to Orchestra or Theater.

## 2. Draw a Decision Tree

Before writing any code, students draft their tree by hand in the Project Planning Guide, same skill as Section 6's Decision Trees lesson, just applied to their own topic now.

## 3. Implement in Python

Here's where the one new piece of syntax shows up:

<div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; margin: 12px 0 16px 0; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
  <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8; line-height: 1.8;">
if ask("Do you like sea animals?"):<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;print("Try a Marine Biology class!")<br>
else:<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;print("Try a Chemistry class!")
  </div>
</div>

<code style="font-size:16px;">ask()</code> hasn't appeared anywhere else in this unit. It asks the user a yes/no question and returns the answer, used directly as the condition in an <code style="font-size:16px;">if</code> statement. Functionally, it works exactly like any other condition students have already written, it's just new vocabulary for how the question gets asked.

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> don't assume students will intuit what <code>ask()</code> does from context alone. A thirty-second explanation up front, "it asks a yes-or-no question and gives you back the answer," saves confusion later.
  </div>
</div>

## 4. Written Reflection

Each project day ends with a reflection section in the Planning Guide, Day 1, Day 2, and a final End-of-Project Reflection on Day 3. This isn't a bolt-on, it's built into the pacing itself.

<div style="background-color: #D5EFFF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-top: 20px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #06338D; line-height: 1.6;">
    <strong>&#8505; Partner feedback is built in too:</strong> Day 2 has students share decision tree diagrams for feedback before coding, and Day 3 has them test each other's finished projects. This isn't a solo project start to finish.
  </div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
